From 719d05dbbdb7f8d910eea0baaebf23bb74f60337 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xavi Ivars <xavi.ivars@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 02:51:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Take clock day and month names from the interface locale
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The clock rendered weekday and month names in English on every system,
which is issue #6360: a session running under es_MX or ca_ES got a bar
reading "Thursday" and a calendar headed with English short days.

Two separate causes. Panel.qml pinned Qt.locale("en_US") on purpose, and
said so: "The interface is English throughout, so day names are not taken
from the system locale." That reasoning was sound while the shell had no
way to be anything but English. The I18n primitive removes the premise, so
the pin now follows the interface language instead, and the comment is
updated to match. Where the week starts is untouched — that is a regional
convention rather than a translation, and it stays overridable.

BarWidget.qml went through Qt.formatDateTime(), which resolves dddd and
MMMM against the C locale no matter what the session locale is. The label
now formats through the interface locale explicitly.

Neither path reads a catalog: I18n.language comes from the environment, so
locale-correct dates work with no language pack installed at all. Falls
back to en_US when no locale is set, which is the previous behaviour.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 shell/plugins/panels/clock/BarWidget.qml | 5 ++++-
 shell/plugins/panels/clock/Panel.qml     | 9 +++++----
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/shell/plugins/panels/clock/BarWidget.qml b/shell/plugins/panels/clock/BarWidget.qml
index 61cf0bf5..da88b4f6 100644
--- a/shell/plugins/panels/clock/BarWidget.qml
+++ b/shell/plugins/panels/clock/BarWidget.qml
@@ -57,7 +57,10 @@ BarWidget {
   }
 
   function formatted(date) {
-    return Qt.formatDateTime(date, activeFormat.replace(/ww/g, Model.isoWeekLiteral(date.getFullYear(), date.getMonth(), date.getDate())))
+    // Qt.formatDateTime() renders dddd/MMMM from the C locale regardless of the
+    // session's, so the label goes through the interface locale explicitly.
+    var format = activeFormat.replace(/ww/g, Model.isoWeekLiteral(date.getFullYear(), date.getMonth(), date.getDate()))
+    return date.toLocaleString(Qt.locale(I18n.language || "en_US"), format)
   }
 
   // ---- Calendar popup. Shape contract for shell.summon/hide/toggle
diff --git a/shell/plugins/panels/clock/Panel.qml b/shell/plugins/panels/clock/Panel.qml
index f0dff3ae..5a4ef43b 100644
--- a/shell/plugins/panels/clock/Panel.qml
+++ b/shell/plugins/panels/clock/Panel.qml
@@ -64,10 +64,11 @@ Panel {
   // convention. Clicking the grid's "W" heading writes the choice back to
   // shell.json.
   readonly property int weekStart: Model.normalizedWeekStart(setting("weekStartDay", null), Qt.locale().firstDayOfWeek)
-  // The interface is English throughout, so day names are not taken from the
-  // system locale. Where the week starts still is: that is a regional
-  // convention rather than a translation, and it stays overridable above.
-  readonly property var labelLocale: Qt.locale("en_US")
+  // Day names follow the interface language, which the I18n primitive picks:
+  // a Catalan interface gets Catalan day names, and an untranslated one stays
+  // English. Where the week starts is a separate question — a regional
+  // convention rather than a translation — and it stays overridable above.
+  readonly property var labelLocale: Qt.locale(I18n.language || "en_US")
   readonly property string nextWeekStartLabel: labelLocale.dayName(Model.toggledWeekStart(weekStart), Locale.LongFormat)
   readonly property var weekdays: Model.weekdayOrder(weekStart)
   readonly property var weeks: Model.monthGrid(viewYear, viewMonth, weekStart, todayKey)
